FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/lsp.rs:957 — The `to` of a plain assignment is misread as a function-literal opener. After `Set` declares a name, the `Set` arm does not consume the following `to` (lines 1049-1094), so the main loop visits it; `is_declaration` is false (lines 918-919), and because `Set` is not in `ends_expression` (597-610) the `else if` at 957-959 pushes a scoped `FrameKind::Literal` plus a scope (960-994). That spurious frame has `closes_at_token` at the line end and then steals the next inline `end` at 744-749 instead of the intended enclosing frame. Repro: `to f(limit)` / `    if limit is 1 then set flag to yes end` / `    say limit` / `end` / `say limit` — the literal frame is popped by the inline `end`, so the `If` frame survives, the function's `end` pops that `If`, and the function scope is never popped; `goto_definition(source, 5, 5)` then wrongly resolves the post-function `limit` to the parameter at line 1 (same failure shape the `else-if` test guards against, but that fixture has no `set`). Concrete fix: treat a `to` whose preceding significant token is `Set`/`Constant` as the assignment operator, not a literal opener (e.g. add those kinds to the `ends_expression` check at 957-958 or track assignment context). VERIFIED: traced the token path at lines 918-919, 957-994, 744-749 against the lexer's valid one-line block form (`if x then say "a" end`, src/parser.rs:3436); not executed because read-only.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1127 — Object-field navigation only resolves during the first pass, using `token_binding[index - 2]` as the receiver; if the receiver is declared later, `token_binding` is still `None` there. The deferred pass that exists precisely for uses before declarations (1163-1193) explicitly skips `member_names` (1176), so a forward reference like `say p.name` above `object p ... has name ... end` never binds `name`: `goto_definition` returns `None` and `find_references` on the `has` field omit the use. There is also no test in tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs exercising `obj.field`/`this.field` navigation at all, so this whole branch is unpinned. Fix: resolve `member_names` in the deferred pass after receivers are resolved (and `object_fields` is populated), and add a member-access test both for forward and ordinary references.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:709 — `analyze` maps lexer failures with `error.message()`, discarding the `Span`; `Error::message()` returns only the text (src/error.rs:172-186). All three navigation entry points therefore return a bare `String` such as `"Unterminated string"` with no line/column, so an editor cannot point at the malformed source. `edge_malformed_source_returns_errors_not_panics` only asserts `is_err()`, so this loss is untested. Fix: include `error.span()`/the full position in the returned message or return a structured error.

4. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1416 — Off-by-one at the delimiter itself: `inside_text` returns `offset > start && offset <= cursor`, so a caret exactly on the opening quote (`offset == start`) is treated as code, and `inside_comment` loops `while index < offset` (1435) so it never inspects a `//` that begins exactly at `offset` (1454); completions are offered while the cursor sits on the string quote or the first `/` of a comment. Fix: use `offset >= start` for the quote and scan up to `index <= offset` for the comment.

5. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1342 — For a builtin (target `None`), `find_references` matches every same-named token whose `token_binding` is `None` (`analysis.token_binding[candidate].is_none()`, 1342-1345), which includes unresolved member accesses (`x.abs`) and type-annotation names (`items of length`) recorded as `member_names`/`type_names`. So references for a builtin name include occurrences that are not uses of that builtin. Fix: exclude `member_names` and `type_names` candidates from the unresolved-name branch.

