19:1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:892-895 — `set` inside a nested block always creates a fresh binding in the current scope, while the interpreter's `set_var` writes through to an enclosing binding (the report itself admits this at `phases/phase-075/REPORT.md` "Known gaps"). For the common accumulator form `set x to 0` … `while … set x to x - 1 … end`, the analyzer declares a new inner `x` *before* resolving the RHS `x`, so `goto_definition` on the RHS points at the inner `set` line instead of the real outer declaration, and `find_references` on the outer `x` misses both the write and its read. This is not lexical shadowing — Redblue has no inner-declaration form here — so the navigation answer is simply wrong. Fix: in the `Set`/`Constant` handler, look up the name across `scopes` first and reuse that binding (marking the token as a reference); only call `declare` when no enclosing binding exists. VERIFIED: traced token order — `declare` at src/lsp.rs:894 runs before the RHS identifier is resolved at src/lsp.rs:931-936, so the RHS resolves to the binding just created.
21:2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:829-832 — a one-line function literal is left open until the end of its source line (`frame.closes_at_newline = Some(token.line)`), but the parser ends the literal at the statement boundary *within* that line (`while self.span().line == open_line && self.can_begin_statement()` at src/parser.rs:1463). For valid source like `say f(to (x) give back x, x)`, the trailing `x` argument is still inside the literal's scope in the analyzer, so `goto_definition`/`find_references` resolve it to the literal's parameter instead of the outer `x`. Fix: close the literal scope at the token where the parsed body ends (the first `,`/`)`/`]`/`}` that cannot begin a statement), matching `parser::can_begin_statement`, rather than at the newline; or derive the close offset from the parsed `Expr::FunctionLiteral` span. Add a test with a reference after the literal on the same line. VERIFIED: traced the parser (src/parser.rs:1455-1469) and analyzer (src/lsp.rs:809-836) paths.
23:3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1189 — `inside_comment` returns true only for `offset < end`, where `end` is the newline index. A cursor exactly at the end of a comment line (and, for an unterminated comment at EOF, a cursor at EOF where `offset == len`) is therefore reported as *outside* the comment, and `completions` returns the whole keyword/builtin/scope list inside the comment. Not covered by `edge_completions_inside_line_comment_are_empty`, which only probes mid-comment. Fix: treat the terminating position as inside (`offset <= end` with the newline/EOF case handled explicitly) and add an end-of-comment / unterminated-comment boundary test.
25:4. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:930-933 — the 5000-line benchmark's wall-clock bound was relaxed from one second to two (`elapsed < Duration::from_secs(2)`), which is the "threshold lowered" gate-weakening class and remains environment-dependent: a loaded CI runner can exceed it, and a genuine complexity regression could hide under the wider bound. The operation is linear, so assert a scaling property (e.g. 10 kB vs 50 kB ratio) or keep a tighter bound with a warm-up rather than doubling the tolerance.
