22:1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1037 — Type annotations are advanced over but never marked as non-references; the outer token loop at src/lsp.rs:981 then resolves every identifier consumed by `skip_type_expression` as an ordinary name. With `set number to 5` followed by `to scale(value: number)`, goto-definition on the annotation `number` returns the variable `number`, and `find_references` from that variable includes the annotation. The only type test (`edge_typed_and_of_parameters_do_not_declare_their_type_names`, tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1152) uses unbound `number` and the builtin `list`, so it cannot detect the collision. Fix: record the identifiers skipped by `skip_type_expression` (src/lsp.rs:952 and :1043) in a set the resolution arm ignores, and add a test where the type name shadows a real binding.
24:2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:967 — `has name` declares the field in the object's nested scope (pushed at src/lsp.rs:753), and `End` pops that scope (src/lsp.rs:686-691), so a property access outside the object never resolves. `docs/GRAMMAR.md:652` uses exactly `set Person.name to "Bob"`; the token `name` there is looked up in the root scope and `goto_definition` returns `None`, while `find_references` on `has name` omits it. No test exercises a property use (the only `has` test, tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1246, asserts the membership negative). Fix: resolve a name following `.` against its receiver's object binding, or keep field bindings reachable, and add a `this.name` / `Person.name` navigation test.
26:3. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:981 — Resolution is single-pass and forward-only: `token_binding` is assigned from scopes built so far, so a use written before its declaration is left `None`. `goto_definition` (src/lsp.rs:1084-1093) therefore returns no definition for a call/use that appears earlier in the file, and `find_references` (src/lsp.rs:1119-1133) excludes those earlier uses from the declaration's answer. This is only correct if Redblue forbids forward references; if a function may be called before its `to ... end`, the navigation answer is wrong. A test declaring a function below its first use would settle it; none exists.
28:4. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:880 — `import network as net` declares `network` as an `Import` first (the `Identifier` arm), then declares `net` in the `As` arm at src/lsp.rs:897. Navigation therefore treats the pre-alias name as a bound import even though only the alias is presumably usable; no test covers the aliased import shape. Verify against the interpreter's import binding and, if only the alias is bound, do not `declare` the original name when an `as` follows.
30:5. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1201 — The string/comment context boundaries are inconsistent: `inside_text` requires `offset > start`, and `inside_comment` scans only while `index < offset` (src/lsp.rs:1220), so a completion request on the opening quote or on the first `/` of `//` offers code completions, while the test matrix claims end-of-comment boundaries are handled and the newline after a comment is treated as inside (src/lsp.rs:1239). This is a small editor-convention gap, not a panic; either document the exclusive start or include the delimiter character.
